# Why publication is a separate pipeline

A literate project has one authoritative source, but it does not have only one useful
presentation. A programmer may need generated source. A reader in the editor may
need a live preview. A website needs linked HTML. A printed book needs pages,
fonts, margins, cross-references, and resources that can travel with it.

These products should agree about meaning without pretending that they are the
same medium. A browser can reveal a fragment on demand; paper cannot. A printed
note can occupy a margin; a narrow screen may need to place it in the reading flow.
The publication architecture therefore shares semantics first and chooses
presentation later.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Snapshot[Captured literate project] --> Parse[Parse the captured snapshot with shared semantics]
    Parse --> Meaning[Shared project meaning]
    Meaning --> Source[Executable source plan]
    Meaning --> Preview[Live editor preview]
    Meaning --> Screen[Standalone screen publication]
    Meaning --> Print[Print publication]
```

The arrows mean that every consumer begins with the same captured text and parsed
relationships. They do not mean that one output is converted into another. In
particular, print is not produced by printing the generated website, and preview is
not produced by reading generated HTML back from disk. The diagram omits host I/O
so that the semantic separation remains visible.

## One source does not require one renderer

The ordinary generation pipeline tangles roots into source files and weaves each
chapter into HTML. Its job is to plan a complete set of project outputs. The live
preview instead reflects editor buffers, including unsaved text, and must remain
useful while an author is correcting an incomplete fragment. Print publication
must reject incomplete structure before invoking expensive converters or external
tools.

A useful way to state the relationship is:

```text
presentation = renderer(parsed snapshot, medium policy)
```

The parsed snapshot supplies meaning. The medium policy supplies choices such as
link shape, page geometry, note placement, and resource packaging. Neither term can
replace the other. Rendering directly from Markdown would lose fragment identity;
rendering every medium through HTML would make HTML layout decisions authoritative
for print.

The separation also prevents generated files from becoming accidental inputs. A
publication command reads the captured literate documents. It does not first write
source or HTML and then publish those artifacts. This matters for unsaved editor
buffers, for deterministic inspection, and for failure safety.

## The renderer-neutral center

Before discussing browser links or LaTeX commands, the architecture needs a form of
meaning that belongs to neither. That center records:

- documents in authoritative project order;
- fragment families in definition order;
- each accepted definition or addition in encounter order;
- source locations and authored languages;
- literal and reference segments within contribution bodies;
- stable targets for families and individual contributions; and
- terms, notes, uses, and their resolution outcomes.

It deliberately does not contain HTML elements, TeX commands, filesystem handles,
or process objects. The print path also retains the parsed Markdown token stream so
it can build a structured document without reparsing source with a different set of
rules.

The implementation calls the fragment-oriented part of this center the
`PublicationModel`. The complete `ParsedProject` places that model beside ordered
documents, diagnostics, inspection data, and shared annotations. These names arrive
after the architecture because they are representations of the boundary, not the
reason for it.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Parsed[Parsed project]
    Parsed --> Graph[Fragment families and contributions]
    Parsed --> Prose[Ordered Markdown blocks]
    Parsed --> Notes[Terms, notes, and uses]
    Graph --> ScreenRenderer[Screen renderer]
    Prose --> PrintBridge[Print document bridge]
    Notes --> ScreenRenderer
    Notes --> PrintBridge
```

Each arrow means “consumes semantic data.” The screen renderer emphasizes links and
interactive navigation. The print bridge emphasizes a complete block tree and page
references. The diagram intentionally leaves syntax highlighting and assets out;
they are later adapters, not additional sources of truth.

## Strict exports and tolerant editing

A useful preview must show the author what exists now, even when a reference is
missing. It may render the unresolved spelling as text and preserve enough identity
for diagnostics and navigation to recover after the next edit. This tolerance does
not authorize a durable export.

Generated publication and print use a stricter rule:

```text
accepted export = complete validation and complete rendering
rejected export = diagnostics and no publication plan
```

The rule is all-or-nothing at the semantic boundary. If a late chapter contains an
unsupported construct, an earlier contribution renderer must not already have run
with observable effects. If one fragment reference is missing, the system must not
publish a book whose index points into an incoherent graph.

Strictness is therefore not hostility to authoring. It is a distinction between a
working view and a claim of completion.

## Screen publication is not merely preview

The live preview and generated HTML share semantic rendering, but their hosts grant
different capabilities. A generated page can use relative links and embed a bounded
exploration client. An editor preview must ask the extension for an authorized
cross-document destination; it cannot invent a durable editor command URI. A
pending destination remains plain text until the host resolves it.

Both forms keep logical targets independent of transport. A fragment family has the
same identity whether its link is a same-page anchor, a relative HTML URL, or an
opaque editor capability. This is what lets indexes and reference overlays agree
without coupling the shared model to either host.

## Print publication is a document transformation

Print begins from the same parsed project but builds one ordered document. Prose,
headings, lists, code contributions, annotations, and standalone resources become a
controlled document tree. Only after complete validation does a trusted converter
produce a LaTeX body. A renderer-owned template then supplies page geometry,
fonts, syntax colors, link commands, and annotation machinery.

This order is important. LaTeX is executable input to a powerful toolchain, so raw
author text must not become renderer commands. The architecture treats structured
document data as the boundary and reserves raw output nodes for trusted renderers.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Meaning[Validated project meaning] --> Document[Controlled publication document]
    Document --> Body[Trusted conversion to a LaTeX body]
    Body --> Layout[Renderer-owned book template]
    Layout --> Plan[TeX and companion resource plan]
    Plan --> Compiler[Private PDF build]
    Compiler --> Publish[Transactional final output]
```

The arrows describe successive reductions in freedom: general literate meaning
becomes a controlled document, then a layout plan, then compiler inputs. The diagram
omits optional browser rendering for diagrams; that work produces bounded companion
bytes before the plan is complete.

## Effects stay at the edges

Parsing, identity construction, annotation resolution, screen indexing, and most
print transformation are in-memory work. Filesystem reads, browser processes,
Pandoc, TeX, and destination writes belong to adapters around that work.

Keeping effects at the edges provides several practical guarantees:

1. the editor and command line can share semantics;
2. validation can finish before expensive callbacks run;
3. resource reads can be bounded and tested independently;
4. subprocess cancellation can be owned by the process adapter; and
5. the previous publication can remain intact until a complete replacement exists.

It also makes limitations honest. External converters are trusted dependencies, a
private build directory is not a sandbox, and a transaction cannot promise that
many filesystem paths change at one physical instant. The architecture narrows
these risks rather than hiding them.

## Why the separation matters

A single renderer would be simpler only by moving complexity into implicit
coupling. It would force editor URLs into durable documents, browser layout into
print, or TeX concerns into parsing. Separate pipelines preserve a stronger unity:
all presentations arise from the same interpreted project and stable identities,
while each medium remains free to honor its own constraints.

The next chapter follows the print branch at its first decisive step: constructing
one validated publication document from ordered prose and fragment contributions.
